<section class="band" id="bring-your-own">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <span class="mos-eyebrow">Beyond the catalog</span>
      <h2 class="mos-section-title">Missing an app? No problem.</h2>
      <p class="mos-body-lg mos-meta">MOS is built to welcome any open-source app. A repository that carries a small MOS package installs straight from its GitHub URL, as one app or as a whole catalog of them.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="byo">
      <div class="mos-panel mos-panel-flush byo-card">
        <div class="mos-panel-band mos-panel-band-accent"><h3 class="mos-panel-band-title">Installing your favorite app is easy</h3></div>
        <div class="mos-panel-body">
          <ul>
            <li>Paste the repository's GitHub URL into the Apps search; MOS shows what it publishes and installs from there</li>
            <li>Add a community catalog the same way: one repository, many apps, kept on your Apps screen under the publisher's name</li>
            <li>Keep a catalog of your own and share it with friends and family as a single link</li>
            <li>Every app from an added source runs inside the same limits as a catalog app: its own storage, its own addresses, no privileged access</li>
            <li>Updates, backups, restart and uninstall work exactly as for any other app</li>
            <li>No package yet? Ask Claude Code: <code>/mos:add-app "help me install Miniflux on my MOS server"</code></li>
          </ul>
          <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/guides/apps/#bringing-your-own-apps">How external installs work →</a>
          <a class="mos-link" href="https://github.com/rpuls/MOS-extra-apps" rel="noopener">See a community catalog →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mos-panel mos-panel-flush byo-card">
        <div class="mos-panel-band mos-panel-band-accent"><h3 class="mos-panel-band-title">Publishing your open-source app in MOS is easy</h3></div>
        <div class="mos-panel-body">
          <ul>
            <li>Add a <code>.mos/</code> folder to your repository: one manifest describes routes, storage, setup fields and health, and your Dockerfile builds the app</li>
            <li>Packaging is one prompt with the MOS plugin for Claude Code, and a complete example repository documents the format for any other assistant</li>
            <li>No store account, no approval queue, no registry: people install from your repository URL and are offered updates when you publish a newer package version</li>
            <li>Their data stays on their own server, the way your project intended</li>
            <li>Publish one app, or curate a catalog of apps you didn't write</li>
          </ul>
          <a class="mos-link" href="https://github.com/rpuls/MOS-external-app-example" rel="noopener">Start from the example repository →</a>
          <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/reference/manifest/">Manifest reference →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
